Best practice is: don’t have the GUI app own a long-lived daemon tree.

On macOS, the clean architecture is usually:

The .app runs as the logged-in user.
launchd owns any long-lived background service.
The app talks to that service over XPC.
Short-lived subprocesses are fine under the daemon, but independent long-lived helpers should usually be separate launchd jobs or XPC services, not ad hoc children.
A good rule of thumb:

If it needs UI or user-session access: LoginItem or LaunchAgent, running as that user.
If it needs system-wide background work: LaunchDaemon.
If it just needs isolation/modularity: private XPC service.
If it needs root/admin power: privileged helper or LaunchDaemon, with the UI app staying unprivileged.
For the daemon and its children specifically:

Let launchd start the daemon. Don’t have your app fork a daemon and leave it around.
Don’t “daemonize” the daemon yourself with double-fork patterns; Apple explicitly recommends launchd-managed processes instead.
Keep one well-defined parent service and treat child processes as workers.
Children should be short-lived where possible and should inherit clear responsibility only.
If a child is important enough that it needs restart policy, crash handling, or independent lifecycle, promote it to its own XPC service or its own launchd job.
Make the daemon as stateless as practical so restart is cheap.
On identity/user model:

A LaunchDaemon is normally the system-style background service choice.
If you truly need a dedicated Unix account, that’s a more “service product” design than a normal app design.
It can be done with a launchd job configured for a specific account, but it’s usually not the nicest choice for app software because TCC/privacy, filesystem ownership, Keychain, updates, and user-session integration get harder fast.
So for most app developers, the best answer is not “dedicated app user,” but:

app as user
background service under launchd
IPC via XPC
least privilege per component
Apple docs backing this up:

Service Management
XPC
Creating Launch Daemons and Agents
Creating XPC Services
Designing Daemons and Services
If you want, I can sketch the best concrete setup for one of these:

GUI app + background sync service
GUI app + root privileged helper
GUI app + dedicated service-account daemon

